<title>USA: RTRS - U.S. issues fiscal 1997 sugar import allocation.</title>
<headline>RTRS - U.S. issues fiscal 1997 sugar import allocation.</headline>
<dateline>WASHINGTON 1996-09-16</dateline>
<text>
<p>The U.S. Trade Representative's office on Monday announced country-by-country allocations for the 1,700,000-tonne (1,873,929 short tons) fiscal 1997 tariff-rate quota for sugar.</p>
<p>The allocation was based on the countries' historical trade to the United States, USTR said.</p>
<p>The allocation of 25,000 tonnes for Mexico was made to fulfill obligations under the North American Free Trade Agreement, USDA said.</p>
<p>The following is the complete list:</p>
<p>    Argentina		69,774</p>
<p>    Australia	     134,681</p>
<p>    Barbados		 11,359</p>
<p>    Belize		   17,849</p>
<p>    Bolivia		  12,981</p>
<p>    Brazil		  235,286</p>
<p>    Colombia		 38,944</p>
<p>    Congo		     7,258</p>
<p>    Ivory Coast	     7,258</p>
<p>    Costa Rica	     24,340</p>
<p>Dominican Republic  285,588</p>
<p>    Ecuador		  17,849</p>
<p>    El Salvador	    42,189</p>
<p>    Fiji		     14,604</p>
<p>    Gabon		     7,258</p>
<p>    Guatemala		77,888</p>
<p>    Guyana		   19,472</p>
<p>    Haiti		     7,258</p>
<p>    Honduras		 16,227</p>
<p>    India		    12,981</p>
<p>    Jamaica		  17,849</p>
<p>    Madagascar		7,258</p>
<p>    Malawi		   16,227</p>
<p>    Mauritius		19,472</p>
<p>    Mexico		   25,000</p>
<p>    Mozambique	     21,095</p>
<p>    Nicaragua		34,076</p>
<p>    Panama		   47,057</p>
<p>    Papua New Guinea	7,258</p>
<p>    Paraguay		  7,258</p>
<p>    Peru		     66,529</p>
<p>    Philippines	   219,059</p>
<p>    South Africa	   37,321</p>
<p>    St Kitts &amp; Nevis	7,258</p>
<p>    Swaziland		25,963</p>
<p>    Taiwan		   19,472</p>
<p>    Thailand		 22,717</p>
<p>    Trinidad-Tobago	11,359</p>
<p>    Uruguay		   7,258</p>
<p>    Zimbabwe		 19,472</p>
<p>All quantities raw value.</p>
<p>USTR also said that 25,000 tonnes of the 47,000 tonnes of refined sugar will be allocated to Mexico and the remainder will be available on a first-come, first-served basis, including 1,656 reserved for specialty sugars.</p>
<p>The allocations to Mexico are subject to the condition that total imports of raw and refined sugar from Mexico does not exceed 25,000 tonnes, raw value.</p>
<p>-- Sydney Newsroom 61-2 9373-1800</p>
